1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,087
From July 1, 2026, new
graduate students in the

2
00:00:04,087 --> 00:00:08,260
US can no longer borrow
federal Grad PLUS loans.

3
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:15,121
Leah, an illustrative student weeks
from her first spring term, has two

4
00:00:15,121 --> 00:00:21,180
private offers on her laptop: 9% fixed,
or variable starting lower, at 7.5%.

5
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:24,178
How much lower does a
variable rate have to

6
00:00:24,178 --> 00:00:26,140
start before that risk has been worth it?

7
00:00:26,967 --> 00:00:32,562
Students already enrolled and borrowing
before July 2026 keep Grad PLUS for up to

8
00:00:32,562 --> 00:00:38,087
3 more academic years, or for the time
left in their program if that is shorter.

9
00:00:39,267 --> 00:00:43,437
New students like Leah still
have the basic federal loan,

10
00:00:43,437 --> 00:00:48,412
a Direct Unsubsidized loan, up to
$20,500 a year and $100,000 total,

11
00:00:48,412 --> 00:00:53,167
with higher limits in professional
programs like medicine or law.

12
00:00:53,847 --> 00:00:58,098
When a program costs more than that
after other aid, the rest can come

13
00:00:58,098 --> 00:01:02,227
from a private lender, and that's
where Leah's two offers come from.

14
00:01:02,907 --> 00:01:06,207
Same lender, same amount,
two kinds of rate.

15
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:09,763
Both are for $50,000 over 10 years,

16
00:01:09,763 --> 00:01:13,140
illustrative numbers, not
a real lender's offer.

17
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:19,173
The variable loan's
first payment is $593.51,

18
00:01:19,173 --> 00:01:24,440
about $40 less a month
than the fixed $633.38.

19
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:29,300
A fixed rate never changes,
so neither does the payment.

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,740
A variable rate follows a market
interest rate, so the rate and the

21
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:37,480
payment rise and fall with it, and
nobody knows where it goes next.

22
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:41,547
The head start is the fixed
rate minus the variable rate's

23
00:01:41,547 --> 00:01:45,460
starting rate; it is negative when
the variable rate starts higher.

24
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:50,532
Leah's fixed rate is 9%
and her variable rate

25
00:01:50,532 --> 00:01:54,940
starts at 7.5%, so her
head start is 1.5 points.

26
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:58,860
A point here means one
percentage point of interest.

27
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:05,500
Real offers differ, so a head start can
be bigger, smaller, zero, or negative.

28
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:10,553
This video replays Leah's
illustrative loan through every

29
00:02:10,553 --> 00:02:14,240
10-year stretch of US interest
rates since January 1954.

30
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:19,970
Each replay moves her rate up and
down exactly as much as the 3-month

31
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:25,360
Treasury bill rate moved in that stretch
of history, and the data is US only.

32
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:32,324
"Worth it" gets one plain meaning
here: how often the variable loan cost

33
00:02:32,324 --> 00:02:37,980
more in total interest than the 9%
fixed loan, and how much more at worst.

34
00:02:38,980 --> 00:02:42,475
Then the replay moves the
head start, from 3 points down

35
00:02:42,475 --> 00:02:45,720
to minus 1 point, to see
where that history changes.

36
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:49,093
By the end there's a
line that any real offer

37
00:02:49,093 --> 00:02:51,540
can be held up against, including yours.

38
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:54,060
This is history, not a forecast.

39
00:02:55,333 --> 00:02:57,593
And the first result looks
like a contradiction.

40
00:02:58,893 --> 00:03:02,713
Leah's variable rate rose above 9% at some

41
00:03:02,713 --> 00:03:06,533
point in 76.2% of stretches, about 3 in 4.

42
00:03:07,353 --> 00:03:13,213
Yet it cost more in total
interest in 14.2%, about 1 in 7.

43
00:03:14,333 --> 00:03:20,083
That 1 in 7 is made of
two very different parts:

44
00:03:20,083 --> 00:03:25,833
28.4%, more than 1 in 4,
for starts from 1954 to

45
00:03:25,833 --> 00:03:31,583
1980, and 3.5%, about 1
in 30, from 1981 on; the

46
00:03:31,583 --> 00:03:37,573
worst stretch, from April
1977, cost $11,219 more.

47
00:03:38,353 --> 00:03:41,153
The reason both are
true is the head start.

48
00:03:42,833 --> 00:03:46,524
Every month the variable rate
sits below 9%, Leah pays less

49
00:03:46,524 --> 00:03:50,653
interest than on the fixed loan,
and that saving builds a cushion.

50
00:03:51,413 --> 00:03:55,025
The cushion grows fastest at the
start, when she owes the most,

51
00:03:55,025 --> 00:03:58,293
so every point of rate is
charged on the biggest balance.

52
00:03:59,173 --> 00:04:03,501
When the rate later climbs above
9%, the extra interest has to use

53
00:04:03,501 --> 00:04:07,633
up that cushion before the variable
loan has cost more overall.

54
00:04:07,633 --> 00:04:11,790
A short jump above 9%
only dents it; emptying it

55
00:04:11,790 --> 00:04:16,293
needs rates that rise early
and stay high for years.

56
00:04:17,393 --> 00:04:21,073
In a stretch where rates drift down,
the cushion just keeps growing.

57
00:04:22,167 --> 00:04:24,587
And the two halves of the
record are very different.

58
00:04:25,347 --> 00:04:30,327
From 1954 to 1980, the Treasury
bill rate mostly climbed, in waves.

59
00:04:31,047 --> 00:04:35,433
It peaked at 16.3% in May
1981, then mostly drifted

60
00:04:35,433 --> 00:04:39,647
down over the following
decades, with big swings.

61
00:04:40,567 --> 00:04:45,807
Same loan, same head start; the only
difference is where in history it began.

62
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:52,286
Loans started in the climbing years
often met rates that rose early and

63
00:04:52,286 --> 00:04:57,067
stayed high, and loans started after
the peak mostly rode rates down.

64
00:04:58,007 --> 00:05:02,847
The best stretch began
in August 1981, near that

65
00:05:02,847 --> 00:05:07,687
peak, and cost $15,295
less than the fixed loan.

66
00:05:08,507 --> 00:05:13,997
It belongs to the falling half,
while the worst, April 1977, belongs

67
00:05:13,997 --> 00:05:19,407
to the climbing half, where far
more of Leah's stretches cost more.

68
00:05:20,467 --> 00:05:24,407
Here is Leah's same loan,
started in April 1977.

69
00:05:25,507 --> 00:05:30,547
It begins at 7.5%, like every replay,
and for a while her cushion grows.

70
00:05:31,287 --> 00:05:34,928
Then the Treasury bill rate
climbs year after year,

71
00:05:34,928 --> 00:05:38,427
and her rate follows it,
to 19.3% at its highest.

72
00:05:39,087 --> 00:05:42,627
The cushion from her first
months is soon gone, and every

73
00:05:42,627 --> 00:05:46,167
further month above 9% adds
to what she owes in interest.

74
00:05:46,827 --> 00:05:50,634
Her payment reaches $863.36 a

75
00:05:50,634 --> 00:05:54,967
month, against the fixed $633.38.

76
00:05:55,747 --> 00:06:00,587
Over 10 years, the fixed loan
charges $26,005 in interest.

77
00:06:01,467 --> 00:06:04,682
Leah's variable loan charges
43% more interest than

78
00:06:04,682 --> 00:06:08,087
the fixed loan, the worst
stretch in the whole replay.

79
00:06:09,007 --> 00:06:11,919
This replay has no rate
cap; a real loan's cap,

80
00:06:11,919 --> 00:06:15,327
if low enough, would have
made this worst case smaller.

81
00:06:16,467 --> 00:06:20,087
So far, every result has been for

82
00:06:20,087 --> 00:06:23,927
one pair of rates, 7.5% against 9%.

83
00:06:24,357 --> 00:06:27,812
So the replay ran again,
holding the fixed rate at

84
00:06:27,812 --> 00:06:31,267
9% and moving only where
the variable rate starts.

85
00:06:32,247 --> 00:06:35,667
Each starting point is a different
offer someone could be holding.

86
00:06:36,687 --> 00:06:42,507
Leah's 1.5 points gives the split
already heard, with her April 1977 worst.

87
00:06:42,507 --> 00:06:48,119
At 2 points, none of the starts from 1981

88
00:06:48,119 --> 00:06:54,005
on cost more, but 20.4%
of the 1954-to-1980

89
00:06:54,005 --> 00:06:59,207
starts still did, the worst by $9,472.

90
00:07:00,387 --> 00:07:05,852
At 3 points, still none from 1981 on,

91
00:07:05,852 --> 00:07:11,169
but 10.5% of the 1954-to-1980 starts

92
00:07:11,169 --> 00:07:15,747
cost more, the worst by $6,033.

93
00:07:17,733 --> 00:07:22,165
With a smaller head start, or a
variable rate that starts higher,

94
00:07:22,165 --> 00:07:26,733
more stretches cost more in both
halves, and the worst gets bigger.

95
00:07:29,913 --> 00:07:34,653
At every head start tested, the
worst stretch began in April 1977.

96
00:07:35,753 --> 00:07:42,413
And at every head start tested, some of
the 1954-to-1980 starts still cost more.

97
00:07:43,513 --> 00:07:47,274
So, back to the question: how
much lower does a variable

98
00:07:47,274 --> 00:07:50,833
rate have to start before
the risk has been worth it?

99
00:07:51,653 --> 00:07:56,497
Measured as how often it cost
more in total interest than the 9%

100
00:07:56,497 --> 00:08:01,493
fixed loan, and how much more at
worst, history gives two answers.

101
00:08:02,493 --> 00:08:07,646
From 1981 on, a 2-point
head start was enough

102
00:08:07,646 --> 00:08:12,685
every time; from 1954
to 1980, even 3 points

103
00:08:12,685 --> 00:08:17,953
was not, and the worst
still cost $6,033 more.

104
00:08:19,233 --> 00:08:23,673
Which kind of history comes next
is something no replay can show.

105
00:08:24,833 --> 00:08:28,982
The replay uses the 3-month Treasury
bill rate as a stand-in for the

106
00:08:28,982 --> 00:08:33,193
lender's index; how it was built is
on screen and in the description.

107
00:08:48,833 --> 00:08:52,840
Leah's illustrative offer
sits at 1.5 points, short of

108
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:56,995
that 2-point mark; at her
point, starts from both halves

109
00:08:56,995 --> 00:09:01,373
of history still cost more,
including her April 1977 worst.

110
00:09:02,373 --> 00:09:05,096
A real offer has its
own two rates, its own

111
00:09:05,096 --> 00:09:07,693
head start and its own place on the line.

112
00:09:08,333 --> 00:09:13,120
It also has what this replay leaves
out: the lender's real index,

113
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:17,833
a rate cap, a grace period, fees,
and the borrower's own budget.

114
00:09:17,833 --> 00:09:21,266
The line doesn't say which
offer is better; it shows

115
00:09:21,266 --> 00:09:24,633
what each head start meant
in each kind of history.

116
00:09:26,073 --> 00:09:27,233
History, not a forecast.

117
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:31,700
Every assumption behind this replay
is listed in the description.

118
00:09:33,060 --> 00:09:35,380
Another replay from this
channel is on screen now.

